relation defined on the set $A = {1, 2, 3}$.\n$R = {(1, 1), (1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 1), (2, 3), (3, 2), (3, 3)}$\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince\n{{field-1}}\n$∈ R$ and\n{{field-2}}\n$∈ R$, but\n{{field-3}}\n$∉ R$, the relation is\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204266 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4470ad24-c221-5b90-921e-3633822b45db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204629 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204629 #edb/ref 1061 136768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "817ab16b-a46d-5955-8708-cb1f47546f3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204630 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204630 #edb/ref 1061 136769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204630 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1762f4ff-dc88-5b0c-9805-520fa3b43039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204630 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204631 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204631 #edb/ref 1061 136772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204631 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b79bb19d-072b-5a55-b055-490dc5d96a87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204631 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204632 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204632 #edb/ref 1061 136773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204632 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0cd41e4a-47e4-57bb-a21b-546af8fcc1ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204632 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204633 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204633 #edb/ref 1061 136776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204633 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dab3ff3f-5ed9-5e06-b684-c1e8180229d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204634 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204634 #edb/ref 1061 136777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204634 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39d5f554-2423-5519-95c8-1637a442da03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204634 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204635 #edb/ref 1061 136780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8460f65a-2290-5c97-87a5-237ff636191c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205242 #edb/ref 1064 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is **transitive** if, for all $x,y,z \\in A$, we have\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y}) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y})$ and $(\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z})$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z})$ should also belong to the relation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The relation \"greater than\" ($\\gt$), defined over $\\mathbb{R}$, is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n- The relation \"are congruent modulo $n$\" ($\\equiv$), defined over $\\mathbb{Z}$, is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$, where $n$ is a fixed natural number.\n\nNotice from the definition of a transitive relation that if the first ordered pair is $(y,y)$, we have\n$(y,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (y,z) \\in R$\nwhich is always true.\n\nSimilarly, if the second ordered pair is $(y,y)$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y) \\in R$\nwhich is always true.\n\nTherefore, to verify transitivity, it's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $(y,z)$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205242 #edb/ref 1065 9081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205242 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ff5ebb7-d081-52f4-b3a5-c24c7fe5d202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205242 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205248 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider some \"nuanced\" cases.\n\n- For any set $A$, the universal relation $R = A^2$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n- The empty relation $R = \\emptyset$ is transitive. Since it does not contain any pairs, the antecedent in the implication from the definition of transitivity is always false:\n$\\underbrace{(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R}_{\\large \\textrm{false}} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R$\nTherefore, the implication is always true, and the relation is vacuously transitive.\n- The relation $R = \\{(1,2), (3,4)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs. So, the antecedent in the implication is always false (similar to the empty case above), and therefore the implication is always true.\n- The relation $R=\\{(1,1), (2,2)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs (i.e., it contains only reflexive pairs)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205248 #edb/ref 1065 9109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205248 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "517981b7-195e-5fc0-9683-e0125c43d8d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205248 #edb/ref 1067 "Special Cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205253 #edb/ref 1064 "A binary relation is transitive if and only if for every pair of \"linked\" arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z$ in the corresponding digraph, the arrow $x \\rightarrow z$ also belongs to the graph. For example, the relation represented by the following digraph is transitive.\n\n![](images/96/96fe8200e2ebd03c23ade6b649a41345f75b66524c188d419ee12ea3c904942b.png)\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" arrows of the form $x \\to y$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $y \\to z$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z{:}\n$$\n\n- Consider the arrow $1 \\to 2$ that ends at $2$. It can be linked with the following arrows that starts at $2{:}$ Linking $1 \\to 2$ with $2 \\to 1$ requires that $1 \\to 1$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$Linking $1 \\to 2$ with $2 \\to 3$ requires that $1 \\to 3$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n- Consider the arrow $2 \\to 1$ that ends at $1$. It can be linked with the following arrows that starts at $1{:}$ Linking $2 \\to 1$ with $1 \\to 2$ requires that $2 \\to 2$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$Linking $2 \\to 1$ with $1 \\to 3$ requires that $2 \\to 3$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n- Consider the arrow $1 \\to 3$ that ends at $3$. It can't be linked with any arrow since none of them start at $3$.\n- Consider the arrow $2 \\to 3$ that ends at $3$. It can't be linked with any arrow since none of them start at $3$.\n\nOn the other hand, the relation that is represented by the digraph below is not transitive since the digraph has arrows $1 \\to 2$ and $2 \\to 4$ but has no arrow $1 \\to 4$.\n\n![](images/04/04ade4abe648bcbd790c6355461fb3f74184667a0a6f4fbbafa8b84859c8c28f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205253 #edb/ref 1065 9118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205253 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60c38e6a-6089-5c3b-ad1a-592edfe5d5d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205253 #edb/ref 1067 "Digraphs Representing Transitive Relations" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
